From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kebab refactor <kebab@local.invalid>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 15:03:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: don't log a repeated handover signal
 as an error

q6v5_handover_interrupt() returns early when handover has already been
issued, after printing at error level. On some SoCs the subsystem keeps
asserting the sideband after the fact -- the SLPI on sm8250 raised it
766 times in a single session on an OnePlus 8T -- so an otherwise
healthy boot logs hundreds of identical error lines.

The early return is the correct and complete response: the handover
callback has run, interconnect bandwidth has been dropped, and nothing
is retried. Demote the message so it stays available for tracing
without reporting a fault that is not there.
---
 drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
index 58d5b85e5..37001b6ea 100644
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -165,7 +165,14 @@ static irqreturn_t q6v5_handover_interrupt(int irq, void *data)
 	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5 = data;
 
 	if (q6v5->handover_issued) {
-		dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
+		/*
+		 * Not an error. On some SoCs the subsystem keeps asserting
+		 * this sideband after handover has already been issued -- the
+		 * SLPI on sm8250 produced 766 identical lines in one session
+		 * here. There is nothing to do and nothing is retried, so
+		 * returning early is the whole of the correct response.
+		 */
+		dev_dbg(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
